<h2>How the calculation works</h2><p>The model separates kept orders from returned orders. Kept orders retain their normal contribution. Returned orders are assumed to refund their sale revenue, incur the entered return-processing cost, and recover only the chosen share of product/fulfilment cost through resale or restocking.</p><h2>How to use the result</h2><p>Use the result to compare products, channels or policies with different return rates. A seemingly healthy front-end margin can weaken when return shipping, inspection, damage, repackaging and unrecoverable inventory are material.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>This is a planning model rather than a full accounting statement. Real returns may involve partial refunds, exchanges, non-refundable shipping, marketplace fee reversals, taxes, varying recovery values and repeat-purchase effects.</p><h2>Example</h2><p>Try the same order economics at several return rates to see how sensitive contribution is to return behaviour.</p>
